  "sourceAssessment": {
    "basis": [
      "jinshu:053:s0169 explicitly names 閻纘 as the person whose words moved the emperor to establish 思子台 in the aftermath of Minhuai’s death and funeral. zizhitongjian:083:s0200 explicitly names 西戎校尉司馬閻纘 bringing his coffin to the palace gate and pleading for 遹, comparing him with the Han wronged heir; s0201–s0202 continue that plea.",
      "The rare identical full Chinese name 閻纘, same Emperor Hui/crown-prince Yu setting and specific advocacy for the wronged heir connect the two records. Tongjian s0197–s0198 explicitly gives 永康元年/300; s0204’s 纘，圃之孫也 continues the same named man. Jinshu’s funeral/terrace sequence is likewise current AD300 metadata. Yan Xun versus Yan Zuan is an English reading difference and does not establish two Chinese-named men."
    ],
    "notes": [
      "Bothcompletecurrentmembers jinshu:053:p066 and zizhitongjian:083:p052 included. Read all37sealedunits/5completeblocks,8claims/5mentions and bothEnglishfields for every owned anchor, including the dates and grandfather statement.",
      "Tongjian’s memorial seeks renewed instruction/restoration before Yu’s death, while Jinshu reports the emperor’s later response to Yan’s words after the funeral. These can be successive references to the same advocate; the proposal does not assert that Jinshu quotes exactly the same speech or invent when those words were first spoken.",
      "The Tongjian phrase 西戎校尉司馬 is the office, not a Sima surname. Its 圃之孫 note remains evidence on the Tongjian record; no new family assertion is added to the extraction or forced onto the Jinshu source. No conflicting Chinese name, role, life date or family evidence appears in the complete owned records.",
      "This is fresh current-source review; old merge/review hashes are not restored or rebound. Both English name suggestions are retained unchanged by the resolution."
    ]
  },
